  "explanation": [
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "This lesson is optional. It helps you read choral music, but you do not need it to complete Grade 5, and nothing in it is in the Grade 5 assessment."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "The most common choir is SATB: soprano, alto, tenor, bass. Music for it is often written on two staves, called short score. The soprano and alto share the upper stave, the tenor and bass share the lower stave. The stems of soprano and tenor notes go up; the stems of alto and bass notes go down."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "When a note is sung by two voices at once it is written with two stems, one up and one down. If each voice has a stave of its own, the music is in open score. In short score the tenor is written at actual pitch in the bass clef; in open score the tenor is often written in a treble clef an octave higher, with an 8 under the clef."
    },
    {
      "kind": "tip",
      "text": "Short score: two staves, stems tell the voices apart. Open score: a stave for every voice."
    }
  ],
